#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443916} :activity/id #uuid "1275d73e-e355-535a-8360-6bc7c66fdc10" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574 :topic/id #uuid "aad2999b-dacb-527f-a4a8-1fce51919fe6" :topic/math-academy-id 4257} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fc19e3-8ca8-5969-9f04-b5b491957e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/01/01776646145493da01e17a764ee1b71161595bcc249f376fc18cd9325a8218e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381688} {:answer/id #uuid "86cdb6bc-b584-57f3-9dbb-3aaf9f6680e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/e4/e43b58f0eb6ca96a0d9849b75b3d72e3100671405ebe6196588cc482acd2cb9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381689} {:answer/id #uuid "af129731-17cf-5410-9081-c5e4ee3cd395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c8/c8b297d8b299fd2ed1c6e82fc8513de2cf30f4b4ad148e4c15d53941c7c40d62.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381690} {:answer/id #uuid "daa7f005-3d69-5915-b55a-e2ecdf47ba3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b8/b879f723061d5565f47bc076a85ab43a91f6981e867d2339061339a771f5043d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381691} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f2c811-72b8-53eb-915c-67d6bafee544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d2/d241c79ec2ab5c8ddc6cc4d1cf33f3355a91c44b30661afcf90e39e1b8ff6969.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381688} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f00ecb4-5a2c-5bd4-86e6-6fc0f7553e8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "96ab9b12-c6bc-56bd-aeba-ea72894263c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-183383" :question/problem "![](images/17/17a493e38294911702a94b8755111db85e7445f03d4d149602b57eebf009f14b.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/e9/e995ce9652d431c39c7a629a5e825c91fcdb2da56b83ad371c6bb319bc548eb9.png)\n\nThen, we sketch the set ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}$ and ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/fa/fa491500bff1c1e9d0f8453552f33b8deed066e3799a36817d039fc625eee47d.png)\n\nAs a result, ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}$ is the following: \n\n![](images/fb/fbcbb5ebd87fef90163ce5a358beb7bc40b4c4b3d91b89684500577ccedf2b13.png)\n\nFinally, to get the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}$ or ${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/01/01776646145493da01e17a764ee1b71161595bcc249f376fc18cd9325a8218e7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "13bbe632-d3a1-5ea4-917d-ac97d5a9c56e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443913 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407606 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3da861c8-066a-5c61-b09e-b19fe4847a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407577} {:answer/id #uuid "55d4b454-b90c-53f1-bb03-1433437d6223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407580} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2e08a4-12e2-56a6-afe0-aaec4835514d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407585} {:answer/id #uuid "9b88c49e-083d-575c-8a5a-f1c566cae7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407588} {:answer/id #uuid "b06266a6-9eff-553d-bee6-4acee85a7d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407577} :answer-field/id #uuid "219bccf4-7364-5184-b837-9a47fc6fccc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "db5f04ef-3cc1-58de-87ac-cce7945d8e35" :question/math-academy-id "q-183355" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\in Z.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\in Z.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. If $x\\in Z$ then $-x\\in Z$, and if $-x\\in Z$ then $x\\in Z.$\n\nII. $x\\in Z$ is equivalent to $-x\\in Z.$\n\nIII. $x\\in Z$ if and only if $-x\\in Z.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\in Z$ if and only if $-x\\in Z.$\n\n- $x\\in Z$ is equivalent to $-x\\in Z.$\n\n- If $x\\in Z$ then $-x\\in Z$, and if $-x\\in Z$ then $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "427c4899-8f2f-5df5-bfb1-7ef58c544fcd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443915}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443914 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443911 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45fbefc3-395f-553e-8152-7832975cb2b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{3,4,8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443905} {:answer/id #uuid "5745fcfd-fe55-53c5-a054-56fdf9cfc204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{4,8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443906} {:answer/id #uuid "85d15c0a-e480-56cb-87dd-9d1c782d2cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{3,6,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443907} {:answer/id #uuid "92df1202-bc4c-5672-b477-6087c1ffb4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{2,4,5,7,8\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443908} {:answer/id #uuid "b2060cd9-9bdc-52df-a275-e3dc56e88953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\{4,6,8,9\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443909}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443905} :answer-field/id #uuid "37dbab6b-7900-50e7-95bc-74222427c1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "eaeaaf30-4952-5f5c-8d39-72353700d469" :question/math-academy-id "q-183375" :question/problem "For the universal set $U,$ given by\n\n$$\nU=\\{2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\},\n$$\n\nfind the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)$ for the following predicates: \n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime.\n\n- $Q(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since we have that\n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\{2,3,5,7\\}\n$$\n\nand its complement is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\{4,6,8,9\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{Q}=\\{3,6,9\\}\n$$\n\n \nand its complement is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}=\\{2,4,5,7,8\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =\\{2,3,5,7\\}\\cap \\{3,6,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{3\\} \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =\\{4,6,8,9\\}\\cap \\{2,4,5,7,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{4,8\\} \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =\\{3\\}\\cup \\{4,8\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,4,8\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bad485c1-18f8-57ef-850a-fe161c531db2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443915 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24f5e0da-6ff9-54f8-9899-0fcb4faf174b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381681} {:answer/id #uuid "6ba148f2-8aed-5afe-b8f6-2d6df1077df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381682} {:answer/id #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6bb8e3-7838-5de4-b233-6ca16778703e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381684} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b87f7-5b3d-5c51-b825-9978e6d5f710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381683} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8661f64-c8fd-5a5e-8d68-13ad71e9e23d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9aa776da-0143-5b4b-9d08-2751ea2726a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-183357" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\nI. $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $5\\,|x.$\n\nII. If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ or if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nIII. If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ is equivalent to $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "cbf2292b-9b8c-565b-b4c1-35f38e115eef" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443914}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1bebf7f-222e-5891-9936-432740b170c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, and $x$ is composite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381667} {:answer/id #uuid "73ad96be-7b66-5c32-81d0-4e3629567422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x$ has more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381668} {:answer/id #uuid "6eec7d7d-14dd-518a-bd3a-7698e21feab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is not composite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381669} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a49ba8-c855-5597-9bac-312a8943d425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x$ has more than three divisors, and $x$ is composite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381670} {:answer/id #uuid "3b717337-2c58-5979-bdc9-3a799fc95640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381671} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdd2d766-2bf1-5ae1-be67-0595eea264b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7487ada-70fd-5ba0-8873-b0b458efd67c" :question/math-academy-id "q-183348" :question/problem "$\\,$ If $x$ has more than three divisors, then $x$ is composite.\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ has more than three divisors.\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is composite.\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x$ doesn't have more than three divisors.\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite."} :step/id #uuid "ecd1d17c-94e6-540b-ace4-f35a9f9b3d94" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186443913}}] :activity/title "Conditional Statements With Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186443904}]}
